::MCR_U06_L02_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A side length changes from 4 inches to 12 inches in a similar figure. What is the scale factor?
{
~A. 2#This does not compare 12 to 4 correctly. The scale factor is new length divided by original length.
=B. 3#Correct. The scale factor is 12 divided by 4, which equals 3.
~C. 8#This subtracts 4 from 12. Similarity uses a multiplier, not the difference.
~D. 16#This adds the side lengths. A scale factor compares matching side lengths by division.
}
::MCR_U06_L02_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A rectangle side of 7 cm is enlarged by a scale factor of 5. What is the new side length?
{
~A. 12 cm#This adds 5 instead of multiplying by the scale factor. Similar figures use multiplication.
~B. 5 cm#This uses the scale factor as the answer. Apply it to the original side length.
~C. 2 cm#This subtracts instead of scaling. Enlargement should make the side longer.
=D. 35 cm#Correct. Multiply 7 by the scale factor 5 to get 35 cm.
}
::MCR_U06_L02_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
On a scale drawing, 1 inch represents 6 feet. How many feet are represented by 4 inches?
{
=A. 24 feet#Correct. If each inch represents 6 feet, then 4 inches represents 4 x 6 \= 24 feet.
~B. 10 feet#This adds 4 and 6 instead of using the constant scale relationship.
~C. 6 feet#This represents only 1 inch. The drawing length is 4 inches.
~D. 2 feet#This divides instead of multiplying. More inches on the drawing represent more real feet.
}
::MCR_U06_L02_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
Which statement best describes similar figures?
{
~A. They always have the same size#Similar figures may be different sizes. They must have the same shape.
~B. They always have the same area#Area can change when side lengths are scaled. Similarity is about shape and proportional sides.
=C. They have the same shape and proportional side lengths#Correct. Similar figures keep shape because corresponding sides share a constant scale factor.
~D. They have no matching sides#Similar figures require corresponding sides. Matching sides are needed to find the scale factor.
}
::MCR_U06_L02_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A scale factor of 1/2 is applied to a 10-inch side. What is the new side length?
{
~A. 20 inches#This doubles the side instead of reducing it. A scale factor of 1/2 makes the length smaller.
=B. 5 inches#Correct. 10 x (1/2) \= 5 inches.
~C. 10 inches#This leaves the side unchanged. A scale factor of 1/2 changes the length.
~D. 12 inches#This adds 2 instead of multiplying by 1/2. Scaling uses multiplication.
}
